INT. NATURAL HISTORY MUSEUM - WHALE HALL - NIGHT

A blue whale skeleton hangs from the ceiling. Moonlight through skylights makes its ribs look like a ship's hull.

Three figures at the base of a display case. Black clothes, soft shoes.

ZOFIA KRAWCZYK-ADEYEMI (40s) kneels at the lock, picks in her teeth. Patient. Her hands barely move.

TRISTAN "TRIX" OKAFOR-LINDQVIST (20s) holds a flashlight with a red filter and a stopwatch. His knee bounces.

MIRIAM SOLOMON-BEAUCHAMP (60s, silver bob) stands at the rail, arms folded, watching the doors.

TRIX
Ninety seconds.

Zofia doesn't answer. The lock clicks. She sets down the picks.

Inside the case: a fossil egg the size of a football, on a velvet cradle.

Trix reaches. Miriam's hand closes on his sleeve. He stops.

MIRIAM
Gloves.

He pulls on gloves. His fingers are wet. They slide. Zofia takes his hands, wipes them on her shirt, helps him.

She lifts the egg. Weighs it. Hands it to Miriam.

Miriam holds it a moment too long. Her thumb strokes the shell.

TRIX
Miriam.

MIRIAM
I know.

She wraps it in a blanket and passes it to Trix. He cradles it against his chest like a baby.

A SOUND. Footsteps on the mezzanine. A flashlight sweeps the hall. Three freeze. Trix stops breathing.

The light crosses the whale. The ribs. The case. It settles on the empty velvet.

Silence. Then the light moves on. The footsteps fade toward the east wing.

Zofia lets out a breath. Trix is shaking so hard the egg rattles in its blanket. She steadies it with both hands.

ZOFIA
(whispering)
Walk.

They walk. Under the whale. Past the stuffed elk. Toward the loading dock.

At the door, Miriam stops. Looks back at the empty case. At the whale.

MIRIAM
Seventy years.

ZOFIA
Move.

Miriam turns. Follows.
